{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_013Js6VDF2s3zwfXmmhwY6Uc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I have a test failure in `ecn_alpha_only_ect1_delta`. Let me re-examine my math.\n\nThe test:\n```\ns.ecn_alpha = 200;\ns.ecn_ect1 = 0;\ns.ecn_ce = 0;\n// delta_ect1 = 500, delta_ce = 0 → frac = 0\n// frac (0) < alpha (200) → EWMA: alpha_shifted = 200*16 - 200 + 0 = 2800\n\n\n```\n\nSo the new alpha should be 2800 / 16 = 175, but I'm getting 187 instead. Looking at the C code, I see the issue—when `delta_ect1 = 500` and `delta_ce = 0`, the condition `delta_ce > 0 || delta_ect1 > 0` is true, so the fraction calculation actually executes rather than staying at 0.\n\nTracing through: `frac = (0 * 1024) / 500 = 0`, then since `frac > 200` is false, we go to the else branch where the code shifts the current alpha left by 4 bits and subtracts the original value to compute the decay. I see the issue now—I miscalculated the intermediate value as 2800 when it should be 3000. The math checks out: 200 shifted left by 4 bits gives 3200, subtract 200 to get 3000, then right shift by 4 to get 187. My test assertion was wrong, not the code itself.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":638,"cache_read_input_tokens":107174,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":638},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"589969e0-25c9-4813-acd8-cf423be18f35","uuid":"04b86f94-09f3-48ac-be61-0d7b238895b4"}
